REQ-NOFLOW-ZRM
When the analysis dictionary contains a "ZermelloTrjNoFlow" block, the
program shall generate no-flow device routes independently of the
flow-following streamlines. In the first implementation, each route shall lie
in a horizontal plane at the midpoint of finite, configured minHeight and
maxHeight values. If variableHeight is enabled, the program shall use a 3D
route constrained to that height range; until 3D routing is implemented, it
shall reject this setting rather than silently use a 2D route.
Implementation: 
    Use the existing startPatches and destinationPatches settings.
Intersect these boundary patches with the route plane. Sample start points
along the start-patch intersections, weighted by segment length, using
nPoints. Require an obstaclePatches list naming boundary patches whose
intersection with the route plane forms closed obstacle footprints. Require a
positive deviceRadius; allow an optional, non-negative obstacleMargin,
defaulting to zero. Expand obstacle footprints by deviceRadius plus
obstacleMargin. For each start point, find the nearest point on any reachable
destination-patch intersection and route to it along the shortest
collision-free piecewise-linear path around the expanded footprints, using a
visibility graph. The route shall not depend on the flow velocity field.
Compute route length from its waypoints and travel time using deviceSpeed,
defaulting to 0.1 m/s; configured speed shall be positive.
    Write successful routes to output separate from streamline results, recording
a stable route ID, start and end coordinates, waypoints, route length, and
travel time. If no feasible route exists for a start, omit its route geometry
and log the unreachable start. The 3D variable-height route algorithm is
future work; enabling it before it is implemented shall produce a clear
configuration error.

Related classes:
- ZermelloTrjGen is the abstract base class. It owns common
  configuration and validation, route-space and obstacle preparation, patch
  intersection and start sampling, destination selection, shared result
  collection, travel-time calculation, and unreachable-start logging. It
  defines the generation workflow and an overridable route-building strategy.
- ZermelloTrjNoFlow derives from the base and implements the 2D visibility-
  graph strategy for shortest collision-free routes in the fixed-height plane.
- ZermelloTrjConstFlow (NO IMPLEMENTATION FOR INSTANCE) is a planned derived generator for a constant-flow
  route strategy. Its flow-dependent route behavior is not specified here.
- ZermelloRouteWriter writes Zermello results separately from streamline
  output.
